\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qc\sb0\sa200{\cf2\b\afs56\ab\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs56
THE BUTTERFLY FRAMEWORK}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qc\sb0\sa800{\cf3\i\afs30\ai\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs30
A Self-Correcting Constraint Architecture for Adaptive AI Agents}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qc\sb0\sa1600{\afs24\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs24
An Open White Paper}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qc\sb0\sa1600{\b\afs24\ab\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs24
Author: RJ Thomas  }
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qc{\cf6\i\afs20\ai\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs20
Released to the public domain of ideas under an open license (see Section 9).}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qc\sb200\sa0{\cf6\i\afs20\ai\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs20
This work was previously prepared as a confidential patent disclosure brief; I elected to release it openly instead of pursuing patent protection, so that others may build on it freely.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
}
\par \pard\plain \s1\ilvl0\outlinelevel0\ql\widctlpar\sb400\sa200\keepn\ltrpar\cf2\b\hich\af5\dbch\af7\afs32\ab\loch\f2\fs32\pagebb{\listtext\pard\plain  \tab}\ls1 \li432\ri0\lin432\rin0\fi-432{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Abstract}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qj\sb0\sa200{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Large language model (LLM) based conversational agents are typically deployed with static behavioral guardrails: fixed system prompts, fixed rule sets, and fixed personality configurations that do not change based on how well the agent is actually performing in practice. The Butterfly Framework is a software architecture that replaces this static model with three cooperating mechanisms that let an agent's behavioral rules and personality profile adapt continuously, in real time, based on a quality signal computed from the agent's own outputs \'97 without retraining the underlying model and without human intervention in the loop.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qj\sb0\sa200{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
This paper describes three original methods that make up the framework: (1) a Positive Reinforcement Constraint System that strengthens, weakens, and prunes behavioral rules based on outcome quality; (2) a Mirror Loop with Personality Drift Correction that periodically checks an agent's trait profile for runaway drift and pulls it back toward a stable baseline; and (3) Quality-Gated Constraint Extraction, an inversion of the usual machine-learning update rule in which good performance suppresses rule-learning and poor performance triggers it. Together, these mechanisms form a closed self-correction loop that runs entirely at the orchestration layer, above the model itself, meaning it can be attached to any LLM regardless of vendor or architecture.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qj\sb0\sa200{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
This document is released as an open white paper. All architectural details, algorithms, and reference implementation guidance are provided so that any developer, researcher, or organization may study, reproduce, extend, or deploy this system without restriction. The author's intent is authorship credit and public benefit, not commercial exclusivity \'97 see Section 9 for the specific license terms under which this work and its reference implementation are released.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
}
\par \pard\plain \s1\ilvl0\outlinelevel0\ql\widctlpar\sb400\sa200\keepn\ltrpar\cf2\b\hich\af5\dbch\af7\afs32\ab\loch\f2\fs32\pagebb{\listtext\pard\plain  \tab}\ls1 \li432\ri0\lin432\rin0\fi-432{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
1. Motivation and Problem Statement}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qj\sb0\sa200{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Most deployed conversational AI systems rely on a system prompt or a set of static instructions that constrain the model's behavior. These constraints are set once, by a human designer, and do not change unless a person manually edits them. This creates a specific failure mode: a rule that is well-suited to one context may be poorly suited to another, and the system has no mechanism to notice this or correct itself. Over a long-running deployment, this rigidity accumulates as a gap between the system's fixed behavioral rules and the actual quality of what it is producing.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qj\sb0\sa200{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
A second, related failure mode appears in systems that give an agent an evolving personality or trait profile intended to make interactions feel more natural or adaptive. Without a correction mechanism, positive feedback loops can push individual traits toward extremes \'97 an agent that is rewarded for asking clarifying questions, for example, may drift toward asking excessive questions in contexts where they are not warranted. Offline retraining can address this over long time horizons, but it is not practical as a real-time correction inside a single deployment or conversation.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qj\sb0\sa200{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
A third failure mode occurs in systems that do attempt to learn new behavioral rules from self-assessment: they tend to extract a new rule from every self-evaluation, regardless of whether the response that prompted the evaluation was actually good or bad. This produces constraint bloat \'97 a growing pile of redundant, overlapping, or unnecessary rules that eventually degrades the very responses the system is trying to improve.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qj\sb0\sa200{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
The Butterfly Framework addresses all three failure modes with a single integrated architecture, described in the sections that follow.}
\par \pard\plain \s1\ilvl0\outlinelevel0\ql\widctlpar\sb400\sa200\keepn\ltrpar\cf2\b\hich\af5\dbch\af7\afs32\ab\loch\f2\fs32{\listtext\pard\plain  \tab}\ls1 \li432\ri0\lin432\rin0\fi-432{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
2. Architecture Overview}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qj\sb0\sa200{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Butterfly sits at the orchestration layer, wrapping calls to an underlying language model rather than modifying the model itself. This is a deliberate design choice: because none of the three methods touch model weights, the framework is model-agnostic and can be layered onto any LLM backend \'97 a locally hosted open-weight model or a hosted API model \'97 without retraining.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qj\sb0\sa200{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
The reference implementation described in this paper was built on a Python/FastAPI backend with a React/TypeScript frontend, using locally hosted open-weight models (in the original build, variants of Llama 3.1 and Qwen served through Ollama) as the underlying generation engine. Section 10 describes how to reproduce this stack from scratch, and Section 11 (Appendix) generalizes the architecture so it can be adapted to other backends.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qj\sb0\sa200{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
At a high level, each conversational turn flows through four stages:}
\par \pard\plain \s29\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033{\listtext\pard\plain  \'95\tab}\ilvl0\ls2 \li504\ri0\lin504\rin0\fi-288\sb0\sa100{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Generation \'97 the underlying LLM produces a response, conditioned on the current active constraint set and personality trait profile.}
\par \pard\plain \s29\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033{\listtext\pard\plain  \'95\tab}\ilvl0\ls2 \li504\ri0\lin504\rin0\fi-288\sb0\sa100{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Quality evaluation \'97 the response is scored against a small set of independent signals (Section 3.3), producing a single quality score between 0.0 and 1.0.}
\par \pard\plain \s29\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033{\listtext\pard\plain  \'95\tab}\ilvl0\ls2 \li504\ri0\lin504\rin0\fi-288\sb0\sa100{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Constraint update (Method 1 and Method 3) \'97 depending on the quality score, existing constraints are reinforced or weakened, and new constraints are either extracted or suppressed.}
\par \pard\plain \s29\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033{\listtext\pard\plain  \'95\tab}\ilvl0\ls2 \li504\ri0\lin504\rin0\fi-288\sb0\sa100{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Periodic mirror loop (Method 2) \'97 every N turns, a separate self-evaluation step checks the personality trait profile for drift and applies a correction.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qj\sb0\sa200{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
These four stages form a closed loop: the outputs of stage 2 feed stages 3 and 4, and the updated constraint/trait state from stages 3 and 4 conditions the next pass through stage 1. No stage requires a human in the loop, and no stage requires updating the underlying model's weights.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
}
\par \pard\plain \s1\ilvl0\outlinelevel0\ql\widctlpar\sb400\sa200\keepn\ltrpar\cf2\b\hich\af5\dbch\af7\afs32\ab\loch\f2\fs32\pagebb{\listtext\pard\plain  \tab}\ls1 \li432\ri0\lin432\rin0\fi-432{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
3. Method 1 \'97 Positive Reinforcement Constraint System}
\par \pard\plain \s2\ilvl1\outlinelevel1\ql\widctlpar\sb300\sa150\keepn\ltrpar\cf3\b\hich\af5\dbch\af7\afs26\ab\loch\f2\fs26{\listtext\pard\plain  \tab}\ls1 \li576\ri0\lin576\rin0\fi-576{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
3.1 Concept}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qj\sb0\sa200{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Behavioral constraints \'97 rules like \'93ask a clarifying question before giving financial advice\'94 or \'93avoid excessive hedging\'94 \'97 are modeled as objects with a numeric strength value, rather than as fixed boolean rules that are simply on or off. Strength determines how much weight a constraint is given when conditioning a response. Constraints are strengthened when they are active during a high-quality response, weakened when they are active during a low-quality response, and automatically removed from the active rule set once their strength falls below a minimum threshold.}
\par \pard\plain \s2\ilvl1\outlinelevel1\ql\widctlpar\sb300\sa150\keepn\ltrpar\cf3\b\hich\af5\dbch\af7\afs26\ab\loch\f2\fs26{\listtext\pard\plain  \tab}\ls1 \li576\ri0\lin576\rin0\fi-576{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
3.2 Mechanism}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qj\sb0\sa200{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Each constraint tracks its own strength, along with running counts of how many times it has been associated with a success or a failure. Two operations govern how strength changes:}
\par \pard\plain \s29\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033{\listtext\pard\plain  \'95\tab}\ilvl0\ls2 \li504\ri0\lin504\rin0\fi-288\sb0\sa100{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
strengthen(amount = 0.1) \'97 increases strength, capped at a maximum of 2.0 so that no single constraint can come to dominate the active set.}
\par \pard\plain \s29\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033{\listtext\pard\plain  \'95\tab}\ilvl0\ls2 \li504\ri0\lin504\rin0\fi-288\sb0\sa100{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
weaken(amount = 0.05) \'97 decreases strength, floored at a minimum before pruning removes it.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qj\sb0\sa200{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Note the asymmetry: the default strengthening step (0.1) is twice the size of the default weakening step (0.05). This means a constraint that alternates between success and failure will trend upward in strength over time, while a constraint needs a sustained run of poor associations to be pruned. This asymmetry is a deliberate design parameter, not an incidental detail \'97 it biases the system toward retaining constraints unless there is a consistent pattern of poor outcomes, which avoids discarding a rule on the basis of a single bad response.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qj\sb0\sa200{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
After every update, constraints whose strength has fallen below 0.2 are removed from the active set entirely:}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\li200\ri200\lin200\rin200\fi0\sb150\sa250\cbpat9\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf12\brsp0{\*\brdrt\brdlncol12\brdlnin0\brdlnout10\brdlndist0}\brsp0{\*\brdrl\brdlncol12\brdlnin0\brdlnout10\brdlndist0}\brsp0{\*\brdrb\brdlncol12\brdlnin0\brdlnout10\brdlndist0}\brsp0{\*\brdrr\brdlncol12\brdlnin0\brdlnout10\brdlndist0}\brsp0{\cf8\hich\af6\dbch\af6\afs19\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs19\loch\f4
self.constraints = [c for c in self.constraints if c.strength > 0.2]}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\sb0\sa250{\cf6\i\afs19\ai\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs19
Pruning step: constraints that have been weakened repeatedly are dropped from the active rule set.}
\par \pard\plain \s2\ilvl1\outlinelevel1\ql\widctlpar\sb300\sa150\keepn\ltrpar\cf3\b\hich\af5\dbch\af7\afs26\ab\loch\f2\fs26{\listtext\pard\plain  \tab}\ls1 \li576\ri0\lin576\rin0\fi-576{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
3.3 The Quality Signal}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qj\sb0\sa200{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Both Method 1 and Method 3 depend on a single shared quality score, computed by a response quality evaluator immediately after generation. In the reference implementation this score is the mean of four independent binary signals measured against the response just produced:}
\par \pard\plain \s29\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033{\listtext\pard\plain  \'95\tab}\ilvl0\ls2 \li504\ri0\lin504\rin0\fi-288\sb0\sa100{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
asked_questions \'97 whether the response appropriately sought clarification where the input was ambiguous.}
\par \pard\plain \s29\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033{\listtext\pard\plain  \'95\tab}\ilvl0\ls2 \li504\ri0\lin504\rin0\fi-288\sb0\sa100{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
appropriate_length \'97 whether the response length was proportionate to the request (neither truncated nor bloated).}
\par \pard\plain \s29\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033{\listtext\pard\plain  \'95\tab}\ilvl0\ls2 \li504\ri0\lin504\rin0\fi-288\sb0\sa100{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
followed_constraints \'97 whether the response is consistent with the currently active constraint set.}
\par \pard\plain \s29\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033{\listtext\pard\plain  \'95\tab}\ilvl0\ls2 \li504\ri0\lin504\rin0\fi-288\sb0\sa100{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
shows_self_awareness \'97 whether the response appropriately acknowledges uncertainty or limitation where relevant.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qj\sb0\sa200{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
These four signals are averaged into a single quality_score between 0.0 and 1.0. This score is the sole input that drives both the reinforcement direction in Method 1 and the extraction gate in Method 3, which keeps the whole system anchored to one consistent definition of \'93quality\'94 rather than several competing ones. Implementers adapting this framework are free to substitute their own signal set \'97 the specific four signals above are a reference choice, not a structural requirement; the requirement is only that the evaluator reduce to a single scalar in [0, 1].}
\par \pard\plain \s2\ilvl1\outlinelevel1\ql\widctlpar\sb300\sa150\keepn\ltrpar\cf3\b\hich\af5\dbch\af7\afs26\ab\loch\f2\fs26{\listtext\pard\plain  \tab}\ls1 \li576\ri0\lin576\rin0\fi-576{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
3.4 Why This Design Choice Matters}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qj\sb0\sa200{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Framing constraint management as a reinforcement-style process at the level of the constraint objects themselves, rather than at the level of the underlying model's weights, is the core novelty of this method. It allows constraint evolution to happen instantly, per-turn, with no training step, no gradient computation, and no GPU cost beyond ordinary inference. It is, in effect, a lightweight evolutionary selection pressure applied directly to a symbolic rule set that sits above the model.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
}
\par \pard\plain \s1\ilvl0\outlinelevel0\ql\widctlpar\sb400\sa200\keepn\ltrpar\cf2\b\hich\af5\dbch\af7\afs32\ab\loch\f2\fs32\pagebb{\listtext\pard\plain  \tab}\ls1 \li432\ri0\lin432\rin0\fi-432{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
4. Method 2 \'97 Mirror Loop with Personality Drift Correction}
\par \pard\plain \s2\ilvl1\outlinelevel1\ql\widctlpar\sb300\sa150\keepn\ltrpar\cf3\b\hich\af5\dbch\af7\afs26\ab\loch\f2\fs26{\listtext\pard\plain  \tab}\ls1 \li576\ri0\lin576\rin0\fi-576{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
4.1 Concept}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qj\sb0\sa200{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Where Method 1 governs behavioral rules, Method 2 governs the agent's personality trait profile \'97 numeric values representing traits such as curiosity, verbosity, warmth, or formality. Left unmanaged, traits that are repeatedly reinforced by the ordinary flow of conversation can drift upward without bound, producing behavior that becomes increasingly extreme in one dimension. The mirror loop is a periodic, automatic self-check that interrupts this drift before it compounds.}
\par \pard\plain \s2\ilvl1\outlinelevel1\ql\widctlpar\sb300\sa150\keepn\ltrpar\cf3\b\hich\af5\dbch\af7\afs26\ab\loch\f2\fs26{\listtext\pard\plain  \tab}\ls1 \li576\ri0\lin576\rin0\fi-576{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
4.2 Mechanism}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qj\sb0\sa200{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
The loop fires on a fixed cadence, controlled by a configurable interval (mirror_loop_interval, default 3), using a simple modular-arithmetic trigger evaluated after every message:}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\li200\ri200\lin200\rin200\fi0\sb150\sa250\cbpat9\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf12\brsp0{\*\brdrt\brdlncol12\brdlnin0\brdlnout10\brdlndist0}\brsp0{\*\brdrl\brdlncol12\brdlnin0\brdlnout10\brdlndist0}\brsp0{\*\brdrb\brdlncol12\brdlnin0\brdlnout10\brdlndist0}\brsp0{\*\brdrr\brdlncol12\brdlnin0\brdlnout10\brdlndist0}\brsp0{\cf8\hich\af6\dbch\af6\afs19\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs19\loch\f4
mirror_loop_triggered = (}{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
\line }{\cf8\hich\af6\dbch\af6\afs19\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs19\loch\f4
    self.message_count % self.mirror_loop_interval == 0}{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
\line }{\cf8\hich\af6\dbch\af6\afs19\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs19\loch\f4
)}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\sb0\sa250{\cf6\i\afs19\ai\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs19
The mirror loop trigger: a lightweight, stateless check that requires no additional infrastructure beyond a running message counter.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qj\sb0\sa200{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
When triggered, the loop performs a structured self-evaluation of the agent's most recent responses, then compares each active trait's current value against the running mean of all active traits. Traits are nudged toward that group mean by a configurable correction rate (15% per loop, by default), rather than toward a single fixed target value. This distinction matters: correcting toward a dynamic group mean preserves the relative differences between traits \'97 an agent that is meant to be more curious than formal keeps that relative balance \'97 while still preventing any individual trait from running away unchecked.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qj\sb0\sa200{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
A representative adjustment inside the evaluation step looks like this:}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\li200\ri200\lin200\rin200\fi0\sb150\sa250\cbpat9\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf12\brsp0{\*\brdrt\brdlncol12\brdlnin0\brdlnout10\brdlndist0}\brsp0{\*\brdrl\brdlncol12\brdlnin0\brdlnout10\brdlndist0}\brsp0{\*\brdrb\brdlncol12\brdlnin0\brdlnout10\brdlndist0}\brsp0{\*\brdrr\brdlncol12\brdlnin0\brdlnout10\brdlndist0}\brsp0{\cf8\hich\af6\dbch\af6\afs19\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs19\loch\f4
if quality_signals.get('asked_questions'):}{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
\line }{\cf8\hich\af6\dbch\af6\afs19\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs19\loch\f4
    self.traits.adjust('curious', 0.05)}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\sb0\sa250{\cf6\i\afs19\ai\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs19
A single trait nudge applied during self-evaluation, prior to the periodic mean-reversion correction.}
\par \pard\plain \s2\ilvl1\outlinelevel1\ql\widctlpar\sb300\sa150\keepn\ltrpar\cf3\b\hich\af5\dbch\af7\afs26\ab\loch\f2\fs26{\listtext\pard\plain  \tab}\ls1 \li576\ri0\lin576\rin0\fi-576{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
4.3 Why This Design Choice Matters}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qj\sb0\sa200{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Two choices distinguish this mechanism from more conventional approaches. First, the correction happens intra-session, at fixed message intervals, rather than requiring offline retraining or an external human reviewer \'97 this makes it deployable in a live conversation with no added latency beyond an occasional lightweight self-evaluation call. Second, the correction target is a dynamic mean computed across the agent's own current trait state, not a hardcoded baseline \'97 this makes the correction adaptive to whatever personality configuration a given deployment has chosen, rather than pulling every deployment toward the same fixed personality.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
}
\par \pard\plain \s1\ilvl0\outlinelevel0\ql\widctlpar\sb400\sa200\keepn\ltrpar\cf2\b\hich\af5\dbch\af7\afs32\ab\loch\f2\fs32\pagebb{\listtext\pard\plain  \tab}\ls1 \li432\ri0\lin432\rin0\fi-432{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
5. Method 3 \'97 Quality-Gated Constraint Extraction}
\par \pard\plain \s2\ilvl1\outlinelevel1\ql\widctlpar\sb300\sa150\keepn\ltrpar\cf3\b\hich\af5\dbch\af7\afs26\ab\loch\f2\fs26{\listtext\pard\plain  \tab}\ls1 \li576\ri0\lin576\rin0\fi-576{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
5.1 Concept}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qj\sb0\sa200{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Method 3 governs how new constraints enter the system in the first place. A naive learning system would extract a candidate rule from every self-evaluation the agent performs, regardless of whether the response being evaluated was actually good or bad. Over time this produces constraint bloat: an ever-growing, increasingly redundant rule set that inhibits the very responses it was meant to improve. Method 3 inverts the usual update rule to prevent this.}
\par \pard\plain \s2\ilvl1\outlinelevel1\ql\widctlpar\sb300\sa150\keepn\ltrpar\cf3\b\hich\af5\dbch\af7\afs26\ab\loch\f2\fs26{\listtext\pard\plain  \tab}\ls1 \li576\ri0\lin576\rin0\fi-576{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
5.2 Mechanism}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qj\sb0\sa200{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
The gate logic is evaluated immediately after the quality score described in Section 3.3 is computed:}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\li200\ri200\lin200\rin200\fi0\sb150\sa250\cbpat9\box\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrcf12\brsp0{\*\brdrt\brdlncol12\brdlnin0\brdlnout10\brdlndist0}\brsp0{\*\brdrl\brdlncol12\brdlnin0\brdlnout10\brdlndist0}\brsp0{\*\brdrb\brdlncol12\brdlnin0\brdlnout10\brdlndist0}\brsp0{\*\brdrr\brdlncol12\brdlnin0\brdlnout10\brdlndist0}\brsp0{\cf8\hich\af6\dbch\af6\afs19\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs19\loch\f4
quality_score = sum(signals.values()) / len(signals)}{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
\line }{\cf8\hich\af6\dbch\af6\afs19\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs19\loch\f4
signals['deserves_reinforcement'] = quality_score >= 0.65}{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
\line }{\cf8\hich\af6\dbch\af6\afs19\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs19\loch\f4
 }{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
\line }{\cf8\hich\af6\dbch\af6\afs19\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs19\loch\f4
# In extract_constraints():}{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
\line }{\cf8\hich\af6\dbch\af6\afs19\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs19\loch\f4
if quality_signals.get('deserves_reinforcement'):}{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
\line }{\cf8\hich\af6\dbch\af6\afs19\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs19\loch\f4
    return []  # Skip extraction entirely}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\sb0\sa250{\cf6\i\afs19\ai\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs19
The quality gate: high-quality responses skip constraint extraction entirely and instead route to the strengthen() path from Method 1.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qj\sb0\sa200{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
When the quality score meets or exceeds the threshold (0.65 by default), the response is flagged as deserving reinforcement: no new constraint is extracted, and the existing active constraints are strengthened via Method 1's mechanism instead. When the quality score falls below the threshold, the self-evaluation text is passed to a constraint extractor, which applies keyword filtering \'97 looking for language such as should, must, need to, avoid, and ensure \'97 and deduplication before adding any new candidate constraint to the active set.}
\par \pard\plain \s2\ilvl1\outlinelevel1\ql\widctlpar\sb300\sa150\keepn\ltrpar\cf3\b\hich\af5\dbch\af7\afs26\ab\loch\f2\fs26{\listtext\pard\plain  \tab}\ls1 \li576\ri0\lin576\rin0\fi-576{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
5.3 Why This Is the Strongest Claim}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qj\sb0\sa200{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
Most machine-learning systems, at any scale, apply updates continuously and treat every observation as a learning opportunity. Method 3 deliberately withholds updates when performance is already good, on the theory that a system which is performing well should have its current behavior preserved and reinforced, not perturbed by the addition of new, potentially redundant rules. Learning is reserved for the cases where something has actually gone wrong. This is a counterintuitive inversion of the typical continuous-update paradigm, and it is the mechanism most likely to be genuinely novel relative to prior art in adaptive AI systems \'97 see Section 8 for suggested prior art search areas if you intend to formalize or publish further analysis of this method.}
\par \pard\plain \s2\ilvl1\outlinelevel1\ql\widctlpar\sb300\sa150\keepn\ltrpar\cf3\b\hich\af5\dbch\af7\afs26\ab\loch\f2\fs26{\listtext\pard\plain  \tab}\ls1 \li576\ri0\lin576\rin0\fi-576{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
5.4 How the Three Methods Interlock}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qj\sb0\sa200{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
It is worth being explicit about how these three methods form a single loop rather than three independent features. Method 3's gate decides, per turn, whether the system is in a \'93reinforce\'94 state or a \'93learn\'94 state. In the reinforce state, Method 1's strengthen() path runs against the currently active constraints. In the learn state, Method 1's weaken() path runs, and the extractor from Method 3 may add a new constraint drawn from the self-evaluation text. Independently of both, Method 2's mirror loop fires on its own fixed cadence and corrects personality trait drift, using some of the same quality signals (for example, asked_questions) as an input to its trait nudges. The three methods share a common quality signal and a common self-evaluation step, but operate on two distinct pieces of state \'97 the constraint set and the trait profile \'97 on two distinct schedules \'97 every turn, and every N turns.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
}
\par \pard\plain \s1\ilvl0\outlinelevel0\ql\widctlpar\sb400\sa200\keepn\ltrpar\cf2\b\hich\af5\dbch\af7\afs32\ab\loch\f2\fs32\pagebb{\listtext\pard\plain  \tab}\ls1 \li432\ri0\lin432\rin0\fi-432{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
6. Discussion \'97 Why This Matters Beyond One Project}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qj\sb0\sa200{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
The three methods described above are not tied to any particular model, vendor, or hosting arrangement. Because all three operate at the orchestration layer \'97 reading the model's output and adjusting state that conditions the next call \'97 they can be attached to a locally hosted open-weight model, a hosted commercial API model, or a hybrid deployment, with no access to model internals required. This is a meaningful practical advantage: it means the architecture can be adopted incrementally, layered on top of an existing deployment, without a migration or retraining project.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\ql\widctlpar\ltrpar{\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\cf0\hich\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs22\lang1025\loch\f3\fs22\lang1033\qj\sb0\sa200{\rtlch \ltrch\loch
The author's motivation for releasing this work openly, rather than pursuing patent protection, is the belief that self-correcting behavioral architectures of this kind are broadly useful infrastructure for the field, not a narrow commercial product. Publishing the full method openly \'97 including the specific numeric parameters, thresholds, and code excerpts \'97 allows any developer or research group to reproduce, test, critique, and extend the work, which the author believes produces more value than exclusive control of the idea would.}
